WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.167 --> 00:00:02.711
It seem like
I can't remember nothing no more.

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:05.380
I try to say what day it is.

00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:06.548
I ain't got a clue.

00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:11.178
I wrote The Last Days of Ptolemy Grey
as a celebration of my parents' lives.

00:00:11.261 --> 00:00:14.556
My father had a kind of cancer
that caused dementia,

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.100
and my mother
had another kind of dementia.

00:00:17.768 --> 00:00:21.104
And so, the reason I'm so happy
to make the 6 episode miniseries

00:00:21.188 --> 00:00:24.983
is because there's a depth to the series
that's very powerful.

00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:27.986
And I feel that seeing this
is gonna have a big effect

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.697
on how you'll see people,
you know, going through dementia.

00:00:30.781 --> 00:00:35.160
What if it's you who can't remember
from one word to the next?

00:00:35.244 --> 00:00:39.748
When you have a loved one
that doesn't know who you are anymore,

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:43.544
it's like the most heartbreaking thing
in the world.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:45.045
I come from a family

00:00:45.128 --> 00:00:48.841
where there are a lot of people
who have dementia or Alzheimer's.

00:00:49.258 --> 00:00:50.634
So I'm kind of surrounded by it.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:54.012
So, I've been trying
to do this project for like ten years.

00:00:54.096 --> 00:00:57.307
I have been told
that you have been forgetting things.

00:00:57.391 --> 00:00:58.934
I remember what's important.

00:00:59.268 --> 00:01:02.479
Walter and I got a call from Sam one day.

00:01:02.563 --> 00:01:06.149
He read the book, and he asked us
what we were doing with it.

00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:08.735
And that, honestly, is how it started.

00:01:08.819 --> 00:01:12.364
Just his love and passion for this story.

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:16.577
I told Walter, "I just need you
to change it into cinematic form

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.705
so we can get it told
in the way it feels from the book."

00:01:19.788 --> 00:01:23.166
Because I've always loved
the integrity of the story.

00:01:23.375 --> 00:01:26.170
I'm really grateful for Sam
for really wanting to do it.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:30.966
Sam has really reached out
for the humanity of this character.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:33.677
You don't know whether
to hate your condition

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:34.928
or to hate yourself.

00:01:35.637 --> 00:01:39.349
You can't translate one art into another
and have it stay the same.

00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:40.684
But what I tried to do in the book,

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:44.021
and I think I might be a little bit
even more successful in the series,

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:47.774
is to humanize people, you know,
going through dementia.

00:01:47.858 --> 00:01:49.902
Old folks ain't scared of life.

00:01:50.152 --> 00:01:51.945
They already got one foot in the grave.

00:01:52.029 --> 00:01:55.407
On the set, people have
really taken the story to heart

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.579
and wanted to figure out
how they could make this feeling,

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:03.165
that I had originally but we all have now,

00:02:03.248 --> 00:02:05.292
how to make that the best it can be.

00:02:05.501 --> 00:02:07.419
I'm just trying
to make things better for you.

00:02:07.503 --> 00:02:09.171
Better don't sound so bad, do it?

00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:10.506
If we want to be better people,

00:02:10.589 --> 00:02:13.592
then it's important for us
to acknowledge that,

00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:16.887
at the end of the day,
we all just need each other.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.472
I want people to be able to
look at this story

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:21.600
and know that
there is a way to care for people

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:24.311
and be gentle with them
in their time of need.

00:02:24.394 --> 00:02:27.606
Wouldn't be no me sitting here
if you wasn't sitting next to me.

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:32.194
When you're having people put that kind
of energy into making the series,

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:34.613
that's really edifying.

